jt-pve-storage-synology (0.6.10-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The other half of taint mode: file operations. A storeid reaches a
    filename in four places and all four sanitised it with s///, which does
    not untaint -- so writing the credential file, deleting it and clearing
    the credential latch all died with "Insecure dependency in unlink" from
    pvedaemon. One Naming::filename_component now does the sanitising and the
    untainting together, and t/13-taint.t covers all four.
  * Show the capacity-units comparison in the READMEs and on the site.

 -- Jason Cheng <jason@jason.tools>  Fri, 07 Aug 2026 12:45:00 +0800

jt-pve-storage-synology (0.6.9-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Untaint every command argument. pvedaemon is `#!/usr/bin/perl -T`, so a
    value read from sysfs -- the multipath map name -- cannot be passed to
    exec: "Insecure dependency in exec while running with -T switch". Every
    operation started from the web interface hit this; every hardware test
    missed it, because qm, pvesm and pvesh are all plain perl with no -T.
  * t/13-taint.t runs under -T so `make test` reaches the daemon's
    environment. Shown to fail on a deliberate regression.

 -- Jason Cheng <jason@jason.tools>  Fri, 07 Aug 2026 12:35:00 +0800

jt-pve-storage-synology (0.6.8-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The host cache flush around a snapshot and a rollback now reports when it
    did not happen. Both dropped the return value, which is the only thing
    that says whether blockdev ran at all -- so every snapshot taken from the
    web interface skipped its flush silently. A rollback now refuses if the
    flush cannot be done; a snapshot warns.

 -- Jason Cheng <jason@jason.tools>  Fri, 07 Aug 2026 12:15:00 +0800

jt-pve-storage-synology (0.6.7-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Resolve every external command to an absolute path. A PVE daemon has NO
    PATH at all -- measured on pvestatd, pvedaemon, pveproxy and pve-ha-lrm --
    so exec fell back to /bin:/usr/bin while multipathd, multipath, iscsiadm,
    dmsetup and blockdev all live in /usr/sbin. The same operation therefore
    succeeded from a shell and failed from the web interface. This is why
    PVE's own plugins hardcode /usr/bin/iscsiadm and /sbin/vgs.
  * multipathd resize map no longer reports "the map did not follow" when
    multipathd could not be run at all, and grow_map stops retrying instead of
    spending the whole timeout on a command that will never work.
  * make check-tool-paths fails the build on any process spawned outside the
    command runner. It found three more on its first run.
  * The probe tool no longer shells out to stty; POSIX::Termios is core Perl
    and needs no PATH.

 -- Jason Cheng <jason@jason.tools>  Fri, 07 Aug 2026 12:05:00 +0800

jt-pve-storage-synology (0.6.6-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fix a disk resize that reached the NAS but not the node. multipathd
    answers `resize map` from its own udev view of a path, which is still
    stale microseconds after the sysfs rescan that changed it — so it found
    the sizes equal, reported success, and left the map short. PVE's next
    step is block_resize, which then failed with "Cannot grow device files"
    and left the NAS and the VM configuration disagreeing about the size.
    The map is now polled until it carries the new size, and the resize
    fails with an explanation if it never does.
  * activate_volume reconciles a map that is smaller than its LUN, so a
    migration onto a node that missed the resize no longer hands the guest a
    device smaller than its own configuration says.
  * Document why Proxmox VE and DSM disagree about the volume size: they do
    not — PVE divides by 1000 and DSM by 1024, and both write "TB".
  * Show the snapshot description in the README and on the documentation
    site, since that is the change 0.6.5 is hardest to see without a picture.

